  "text": "Water held a sacred place for the Celts. This held for rivers and springs as much as lakes and wells. Nature was the seat of their gods. Water spread life and kept the land in good worth. Water drew the line between this world and the Otherworld. Fairies kept their cities under the lakes or on the river isles. Healing power often followed the water of the holy wells. The Scottish Water Bull, called Tarbh Uisge, held no warmth for folk. It stopped at the gates of the Fairy Mounds. The Water Horse rose from the reach of the sea. People named it Aughisky, Eachuisge, or Each Uisge. The beast looked like the strongest horse a rider could meet. A fool who mounted the horse came to quick regret. The horse galloped until the rider lost breath below. Some water horses fed at river fords where no bridge crossed. A traveler mounted the horse to keep a dry foot. The horse dove at mid-river and tried to drown the rider. One rule alone kept a rider alive. The horse must always face away from the water. The brute changed shape with ease. It might take the form of a lone maid or a young dandy.",
